WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.215
Lesson 4.4.

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:04.520
Restaking & shared security.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.642
By the end, you'll be able to map

00:00:06.642 --> 00:00:09.824
every extra risk layer restaking adds, before ever

00:00:09.824 --> 00:00:12.195
accepting its extra yield in exchange.

00:00:13.495 --> 00:00:14.785
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.785 --> 00:00:17.764
What restaking actually reuses, and why services

00:00:17.764 --> 00:00:20.010
are willing to pay extra for it.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.605
The two new risks this specifically adds:

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:25.685
extra slashing conditions, and operator risk.

00:00:25.685 --> 00:00:28.897
Correlated failure, what happens when one collateral

00:00:28.897 --> 00:00:31.318
base secures many services at once.

00:00:31.318 --> 00:00:33.565
And a full worked example, stacking up

00:00:33.565 --> 00:00:36.158
the source material's own six-layer scenario.

00:00:37.458 --> 00:00:39.393
What restaking actually reuses.

00:00:40.693 --> 00:00:42.681
Here's what restaking actually does

00:00:42.681 --> 00:00:44.858
with assets you've already staked.

00:00:44.858 --> 00:00:48.247
Instead of that stake securing only its original

00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:51.982
network, restaking reuses the exact same staked value

00:00:51.982 --> 00:00:55.025
to secure additional services on top of it,

00:00:55.025 --> 00:00:59.106
things like oracles, bridges, or other infrastructure that

00:00:59.106 --> 00:01:02.518
also need real economic security behind them.

00:01:02.518 --> 00:01:05.429
In exchange for taking on that extra duty, and

00:01:05.429 --> 00:01:08.649
its risk, you're paid extra rewards, and very often

00:01:08.649 --> 00:01:11.498
points, on top of your original staking yield.

00:01:12.798 --> 00:01:16.308
Worth being precise about why these additional services are

00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:18.881
willing to pay for this at all, rather than

00:01:18.881 --> 00:01:21.989
simply building their own security from scratch.

00:01:21.989 --> 00:01:24.866
Bootstrapping genuine economic security from

00:01:24.866 --> 00:01:27.084
nothing is slow and expensive.

00:01:27.084 --> 00:01:30.529
Renting already-staked, already-trusted value is faster,

00:01:30.529 --> 00:01:32.944
and often cheaper, for the service.

00:01:32.944 --> 00:01:35.146
That's a completely reasonable trade for

00:01:35.146 --> 00:01:36.574
them to want to make.

00:01:36.574 --> 00:01:38.248
It doesn't automatically make it

00:01:38.248 --> 00:01:39.314
a good trade for you.

00:01:40.614 --> 00:01:43.274
Here's the actual trade-off, side by side,

00:01:43.274 --> 00:01:45.811
between leaving a stake exactly where it

00:01:45.811 --> 00:01:48.029
is versus restaking it further.

00:01:48.029 --> 00:01:51.717
Plain staking carries one network's own slashing and downtime

00:01:51.717 --> 00:01:54.870
risk, already covered back in Lesson four point two,

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.074
and nothing more on top of that.

00:01:57.074 --> 00:02:01.811
Restaking adds every additional service's own slashing conditions and

00:02:01.811 --> 00:02:05.330
operator risk, stacked directly on top of that same

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:08.849
original exposure, in exchange for the extra reward.

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:11.239
Quick check.

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:13.383
What does restaking actually add,

00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:15.274
in exchange for extra rewards?

00:02:19.694 --> 00:02:23.473
The answer: extra slashing conditions, and operator risk, on

00:02:23.473 --> 00:02:27.004
top of whatever risk your original stake already carried.

00:02:28.304 --> 00:02:30.139
Two new risks, stacked on top.

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:34.527
Here's exactly what each of these two risks actually

00:02:34.527 --> 00:02:37.914
means, since they compound rather than simply repeat.

00:02:37.914 --> 00:02:40.708
Every additional service you help secure comes

00:02:40.708 --> 00:02:43.205
with its own slashing conditions, its own

00:02:43.205 --> 00:02:46.059
specific rules for what counts as misbehaviour,

00:02:46.059 --> 00:02:48.499
and its own specific penalty for it.

00:02:48.499 --> 00:02:51.387
And every service also depends on an operator,

00:02:51.387 --> 00:02:55.196
the entity actually running that service's infrastructure day

00:02:55.196 --> 00:02:58.452
to day, whose own competence and reliability becomes

00:02:58.452 --> 00:02:59.804
part of your risk too.

00:03:01.104 --> 00:03:04.089
Worth being precise about how these risks actually

00:03:04.089 --> 00:03:07.250
stack across multiple services, since it isn't simply

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.009
additive in the way it might first appear.

00:03:10.009 --> 00:03:13.645
Securing four separate services doesn't just mean four

00:03:13.645 --> 00:03:17.184
separate, independent risks sitting side by side.

00:03:17.184 --> 00:03:19.923
It means four separate ways your one underlying

00:03:19.923 --> 00:03:22.148
stake can be slashed, any one of which

00:03:22.148 --> 00:03:24.602
alone is enough to cost you, regardless of

00:03:24.602 --> 00:03:26.884
how well the other three are performing.

00:03:28.184 --> 00:03:29.274
Quick check.

00:03:29.274 --> 00:03:31.382
What are the two new risks restaking

00:03:31.382 --> 00:03:34.059
specifically stacks on top of ordinary staking?

00:03:38.479 --> 00:03:42.285
The answer: each service's own slashing conditions, and the

00:03:42.285 --> 00:03:45.583
operator risk of whoever actually runs that service.

00:03:46.883 --> 00:03:48.043
Correlated failure.

00:03:49.343 --> 00:03:52.248
Here's exactly why correlated failure is the

00:03:52.248 --> 00:03:55.346
real structural concern underneath all of this,

00:03:55.346 --> 00:03:58.228
not just a restatement of ordinary risk.

00:03:58.228 --> 00:04:00.577
One single collateral base ends up

00:04:00.577 --> 00:04:04.098
securing many different services simultaneously.

00:04:04.098 --> 00:04:06.955
That collateral base isn't duplicated for each

00:04:06.955 --> 00:04:09.873
service; it's the same underlying value, shared

00:04:09.873 --> 00:04:11.693
across every one of them.

00:04:11.693 --> 00:04:14.834
A serious failure in any single service can therefore

00:04:14.834 --> 00:04:17.858
reach back and hit that same shared collateral, and

00:04:17.858 --> 00:04:20.883
through it, every other service depending on it too.

00:04:22.183 --> 00:04:25.130
Worth being precise about what this actually means

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.193
for how you should think about diversification here,

00:04:28.193 --> 00:04:31.093
since the usual instinct doesn't quite apply.

00:04:31.093 --> 00:04:34.413
Securing four services with the same underlying

00:04:34.413 --> 00:04:37.526
stake isn't the same kind of diversification

00:04:37.526 --> 00:04:40.108
as holding four unrelated assets.

00:04:40.108 --> 00:04:42.431
It's closer to four separate ways one

00:04:42.431 --> 00:04:44.815
single point can fail, all sharing the

00:04:44.815 --> 00:04:47.138
exact same foundation underneath them.

00:04:48.438 --> 00:04:50.298
Tracing one failure through the stack.

00:04:51.598 --> 00:04:55.238
Here's that mechanism traced through concretely, one step at

00:04:55.238 --> 00:04:58.163
a time, for a single service out of several.

00:04:58.163 --> 00:05:01.280
One of the four services you're securing suffers a

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.552
genuine slashing event, triggered entirely by its own conditions.

00:05:05.552 --> 00:05:08.294
That slashing draws directly against the shared

00:05:08.294 --> 00:05:11.378
collateral underneath it, the same collateral backing

00:05:11.378 --> 00:05:13.392
your other three services too.

00:05:13.392 --> 00:05:16.230
Your position across all four services is now

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:18.883
thinner than it was, even though the other

00:05:18.883 --> 00:05:22.277
three services themselves did absolutely nothing wrong.

00:05:23.577 --> 00:05:26.244
Here's how to actually read a service's slashing

00:05:26.244 --> 00:05:29.102
conditions yourself, before you ever secure it.

00:05:29.102 --> 00:05:33.448
Find its own published documentation specifically on what triggers

00:05:33.448 --> 00:05:36.797
a penalty, not just its advertised reward rate.

00:05:36.797 --> 00:05:38.943
Check whether the penalty is a small,

00:05:38.943 --> 00:05:40.863
capped amount, or can scale up to

00:05:40.863 --> 00:05:43.027
a much larger share of the stake.

00:05:43.027 --> 00:05:45.210
And check how the service's own operator

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.084
has actually performed historically, since that track

00:05:48.084 --> 00:05:50.107
record is what you're really trusting.

00:05:51.407 --> 00:05:52.412
Worked example.

00:05:53.712 --> 00:05:56.056
Here's the full stack, exactly as the

00:05:56.056 --> 00:05:58.401
source material lays it out, layer by

00:05:58.401 --> 00:06:01.107
layer, for a single restaking position.

00:06:01.107 --> 00:06:05.033
E.T.H., staked to become native staked E.T.H. That

00:06:05.033 --> 00:06:08.567
becomes an L.S.T., a liquid staking token.

00:06:08.567 --> 00:06:11.811
The L.S.T. is deposited into a restaking

00:06:11.811 --> 00:06:15.592
protocol, becoming a liquid restaking token.

00:06:15.592 --> 00:06:17.983
That restaking protocol then secures

00:06:17.983 --> 00:06:20.222
four separate services on top.

00:06:20.222 --> 00:06:22.785
Count them up, and that's six layers, total,

00:06:22.785 --> 00:06:25.292
each one of them independently able to fail.

00:06:26.592 --> 00:06:29.193
Here's the one sentence this entire worked

00:06:29.193 --> 00:06:31.855
example exists to prove, worth holding onto

00:06:31.855 --> 00:06:34.514
more than the specific count of layers.

00:06:34.514 --> 00:06:37.857
The extra yield restaking offers has to genuinely pay

00:06:37.857 --> 00:06:40.704
for every single one of those six layers, not

00:06:40.704 --> 00:06:44.099
just the headline risk you happen to notice first.

00:06:44.099 --> 00:06:45.802
If it doesn't, you're not being paid

00:06:45.802 --> 00:06:47.689
enough for what you've actually taken on.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.790
Here's specifically why points deserve their own

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:54.419
separate treatment in this stack, rather than

00:06:54.419 --> 00:06:57.005
being counted as guaranteed extra yield.

00:06:57.005 --> 00:06:59.242
A stated A.P.Y. is a number the

00:06:59.242 --> 00:07:02.528
protocol is actually committing to, right now,

00:07:02.528 --> 00:07:05.205
however it ultimately gets funded.

00:07:05.205 --> 00:07:08.373
Points are, at best, a claim on some future,

00:07:08.373 --> 00:07:12.103
unconfirmed reward, of unknown size, that may or may

00:07:12.103 --> 00:07:15.060
not ever materialise into anything at all.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:17.450
Quick check.

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:19.952
How should points be valued, specifically, when

00:07:19.952 --> 00:07:22.245
you're planning around a restaking position?

00:07:26.665 --> 00:07:28.440
The answer: at zero.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:31.311
Treat any actual payout as a pleasant surprise,

00:07:31.311 --> 00:07:33.285
never as part of the plan itself.

00:07:34.585 --> 00:07:36.830
What 'speculative bucket' actually means.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:41.253
Worth being precise about what sizing something in

00:07:41.253 --> 00:07:44.928
your speculative bucket actually means, in practice, rather

00:07:44.928 --> 00:07:47.310
than treating it as a vague label.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:50.797
The real test is simple: could this entire position

00:07:50.797 --> 00:07:53.747
go to zero, all six layers at once, without

00:07:53.747 --> 00:07:56.930
changing a single one of your actual plans.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:59.542
If the honest answer is no, the position is

00:07:59.542 --> 00:08:02.510
currently sized too large for what it actually is.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:05.270
Here's your checklist.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.310
Do it before restaking anything.

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:10.328
Write down every single layer of your own stack,

00:08:10.328 --> 00:08:13.585
explicitly, the way the worked example just did.

00:08:13.585 --> 00:08:17.500
Read the actual slashing conditions of every service in

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:21.645
that stack, not just the headline A.P.Y. it advertises.

00:08:21.645 --> 00:08:24.777
And size the entire position inside your speculative

00:08:24.777 --> 00:08:28.440
bucket specifically, never inside capital you're depending on.

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:32.738
Here's the question this entire lesson reduces

00:08:32.738 --> 00:08:34.652
to, in the same spirit as the

00:08:34.652 --> 00:08:38.205
yield-farming question from earlier in this module.

00:08:38.205 --> 00:08:41.212
Would you take on six separate, stacked failure

00:08:41.212 --> 00:08:43.906
points, each capable of costing you on its

00:08:43.906 --> 00:08:47.665
own, for whatever this specific restaking position actually

00:08:47.665 --> 00:08:51.030
pays, once points are correctly valued at zero?

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:52.819
If the honest answer is no, the

00:08:52.819 --> 00:08:55.222
extra yield isn't actually paying for what

00:08:55.222 --> 00:08:56.620
it's asking you to carry.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.165
Let's recap.

00:08:59.165 --> 00:09:03.594
Restaking reuses your already-staked assets to secure additional

00:09:03.594 --> 00:09:06.960
services, for extra rewards and often points.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.331
Each new service adds its own slashing

00:09:09.331 --> 00:09:11.824
conditions and its own operator risk, on

00:09:11.824 --> 00:09:14.070
top of what you already carried.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:17.547
One shared collateral base securing many services

00:09:17.547 --> 00:09:21.810
creates correlated failure, not ordinary diversification.

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:25.128
And points get valued at zero when planning, since

00:09:25.128 --> 00:09:28.185
only the stated A.P.Y. is an actual commitment.

00:09:29.485 --> 00:09:32.125
Write down every layer of your stack, read

00:09:32.125 --> 00:09:35.257
each service's slashing conditions, and size it in

00:09:35.257 --> 00:09:38.750
your speculative bucket, with points valued at zero.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:42.860
Next up, Lesson 4.5: vaults and yield optimisers.
